  (development                                                       23.8%)

A nominal 5% test that rejects 38.6% of the time when its null is true is not a
5% test. The cause is that BOTH regressors are measured with error: when
sigma_MAP carries independent noise, sigma_base genuinely does hold information
about |r| beyond it, and the coefficient is positive with no compression present
at all. The regression cannot separate "M.A.P. compresses the cross-section"
from "M.A.P.'s sigma is noisy", because both make sigma_base incrementally
informative.

  A CORRECTION TO THE PREMISE I WAS GIVEN AND ACCEPTED. Measurement error in
  log sigma_baseline does attenuate its coefficient toward zero, which is
  conservative -- that much is right. But measurement error in log sigma_MAP
  pushes the same coefficient AWAY from zero, and in this panel that effect
  dominates: the net is anti-conservative by roughly eight-fold at the 5% level.
  The test is conservative in one of its two error channels and badly
  anti-conservative overall.

  So the rule that retired S4 retires this one too, one step earlier: the power
  was computed before registration, and it revealed a size problem rather than a
  power problem. Recorded as the reason it is NOT registered.

  WHAT IT COULD HONESTLY TEST, if someone wants it later: the weaker and still
  useful claim that sigma_base is incrementally informative about |r| given
  sigma_MAP -- i.e. that a COMBINED volatility would beat M.A.P.'s alone. That is
  a statement about forecast combination and it is true under either mechanism,
  which is exactly why it is testable here and "compression" is not. It would
  need its own out-of-sample split to avoid fitting the combination on the data
  that judges it, and it is not part of any registered plan.

No test of the compression MECHANISM is registered. S1, S2 and S3 test whether
the compression PATTERN replicates, which they do validly, and record 7 already
established that no registered test distinguishes the mechanism behind the tails.

