PART C — the alternative, stated honestly, and why the five-consecutive rule
covers it -- demonstrated rather than argued
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE HONEST OBJECTION: if DNS failures never charge the cumulative allowance, a
systematically broken resolver could fail every item in the band, each one
retried, each one charging nothing, and the run would grind through 353 items
with no halt pressure at all. A rule that excuses an entire failure class can be
consumed by that class.

ADR 0019 anticipated exactly this and gave it a different instrument:

  | consecutive failures | 5 | the inference server down, a provider revoked,
    disk full -- a state that will not fix itself |
  | cumulative failures in a band | 2% (approx. 7 of 365) | slow attrition that
    would still leave the corpus materially incomplete |

  "Five consecutive is a strong systematic signal: at any plausible per-item
   failure rate, an unbroken run of five is far more likely to be one cause than
   five coincidences."

The two thresholds catch different things ON PURPOSE. The cumulative cap is for
scattered attrition, where each failure is its own small fact; the consecutive
rule is for one cause hitting everything, where the count is irrelevant because
the run of them is the evidence. A broken resolver is the second kind by
definition -- it does not fail every fifth item, it fails everything until it is
fixed -- so it trips the instrument built for it long before the count would
matter.

THIS IS NOT A THEORETICAL DEFENCE. It happened today, and it worked:

  2026-09-02 15:47:08   HALTED -- halted on 5 consecutive failures;
                        failures by reason: exhibit_error=1, exhibit_unreachable=5

At the halt the progress line read `failed=6` against an allowance of 8. The
cumulative counter had charged those transient failures (Part B) and STILL had
not reached its limit. The consecutive rule fired first, after five items, and
stopped the band. The resolver fault consumed five items, not three hundred.

So the answer to the objection is: the class-exclusion cannot be consumed,
because the failure mode that would consume it is the one the other threshold is
for, and that threshold demonstrably fires first and fires early.

