resolved by recurrence, not abandoned after one. Closing the ambiguous band with
an item on one strike would apply a shorter rule to the second band than to the
first, and the two bands are about to be COMPARED. A leakage estimate is a
difference between them, so any asymmetry in how they were produced lands
directly in the number this phase exists for.

WHY THIS IS NOT AN OUTCOME-DRIVEN CHOICE, and how that is checkable:

  - It is recorded here BEFORE the retry runs and before anything is scored. The
    git note is timestamped and the ledger shows no scoring has occurred.
  - The decision is symmetric in EVR's outcome. If EVR completes, the band closes
    at 350 complete. If it fails again, the repeat rule resolves it and the band
    closes at 349 complete with 4 terminal failures. BOTH outcomes close the
    band; neither triggers a further retry, and no third option exists.
  - Either way the failure allowance is untouched: 4 permanent failures against
    an allowance of 8, or 3 against 8. The retry cannot rescue a halt because
    there is no halt to rescue.
  - I do not know, and cannot know, whether EVR completing helps or hurts the
    leakage estimate. One additional item out of 349 moves no interval
    materially, and its direction is unknowable in advance.

WHAT WOULD HAVE MADE THIS SELF-SERVING, stated so the distinction is legible: a
retry chosen after seeing that the band was one item short of some threshold, or
after a first score came back badly, or applied selectively to items expected to
help. None of those is the case, and the ordering in this repository is the
evidence rather than the assertion.

AFTER THE RETRY THE BAND IS CLOSED. No further resumption, no further retries,
whatever EVR does.

