-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART B — THE HOLDOUT WILL BE SPENT. Decided by the operator, having seen the fit.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
STATED PLAINLY BECAUSE IT IS THE KIND OF THING THAT IS USUALLY BURIED: the
pre-registration is SILENT on whether a failed development condition still spends
the holdout. ADR 0032's only sentence about sequencing -- "Phase 3 fits a and b on
the development half by minimising its log score, then reports the holdout once" --
is unconditional on its face. Record 3 defines what FAILS means and says nothing
about the consequence. ADR 0031 governs how the holdout is spent, not whether.
No conditional statement exists anywhere in either ADR or in records 1-11.

So this decision could not be made by consulting the record, and it was made with
the development fit already visible. That is written down rather than smoothed
over.

THE DECISION: the holdout is spent, on the pre-registered correction, regardless
of the failed success condition.

THE REASONING: declining to spend would condition the holdout on a development
result. That is precisely the selection the holdout exists to prevent. A holdout
spent only when the fit looks good is not a holdout -- it is a second development
set with a publication filter, and the asymmetry would be invisible in the
reported number. Spending it unconditionally costs a possibly-uninformative test;
declining costs the instrument's meaning.

Note also what is NOT being consumed: THE AMBIGUOUS BAND REMAINS UNUSED FOR
CALIBRATION. It was drawn for the leakage estimate, which is now reported, and no
part of Phase 3 fits anything on it. It is therefore available to test a future
per-item volatility map -- the successor named in record 6 Part C -- on a sample
that has never had a correction fitted to it. That successor is new work with its
own ADR and is not being started here.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART C — the success condition FAILED, and a specification gap decided it
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Fitted on the development half, log-score objective, closed form (the objective
makes this the normal MLE, so there is no optimiser to misconfigure):

    a = -0.0786   cluster-robust CI [-0.4334, +0.1669]   covers 0, as predicted
    b =  1.3310   cluster-robust CI [ 1.2425,  1.4969]   excludes 1

    corrected MAD-scale of z:  1.1052 -> 0.8304

    parameters REFITTED per resample   CI [0.6981, 0.9981]   EXCLUDES 1.0 -> FAIL
    parameters held at the estimate    CI [0.6993, 1.0623]   covers 1.0

VERDICT: FAILED, on the refitted reading. Margin 0.0019.

**A SPECIFICATION GAP DECIDED THIS, NOT THE DATA.** Record 3 said "judged by the
cluster-robust 95% CI from the same moving-block bootstrap" and did not say whether
a and b are refitted inside each resample. The two readings disagree, and the
distance between the failing bound and 1.0 is smaller than the distance between
the two readings.

BOTH ESTIMANDS ARE LEGITIMATE AND THEY ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS:

  FIXED-PARAMETER reading -- describes WHAT HAPPENS NEXT. The holdout will be
  scored with a and b fixed at the development estimates; it does not refit. So
  the fixed reading is the one whose estimand matches the operation actually
  about to be performed.

  REFITTED reading -- describes WHETHER THE PROCEDURE CALIBRATES on a fresh
  sample. It carries the uncertainty in a and b, which is real and which the
  fixed reading omits.

Neither was specified, and choosing between them now is choosing with the answer
visible. The call recorded here is FAILED on the refitted reading, because that is
the treatment that accounts for parameter uncertainty and the one that would have
been chosen before seeing the numbers -- but the fixed reading's estimand argument
is recorded beside it because it is the better description of the holdout step,
and a reader should not have to reconstruct it.

SECOND NEAR-MISS THIS FORTNIGHT of the same kind: a pre-registered threshold that
did not specify enough to be evaluated without a fresh judgement. The first was
ADR 0032's original A2 condition, which named the 2.492 iid critical value on a
panel with 18 clusters and had to be replaced by record 3. Both were written
carefully, both looked complete, and both needed a decision at evaluation time
that the author had not realised was open.

The generalisable form: A THRESHOLD IS NOT A PRE-REGISTRATION UNLESS THE ESTIMATOR
IS ALSO PRE-REGISTERED. Naming the statistic, the level and the direction is not
enough; the resampling scheme, what is refitted inside it, and the null all have to
be fixed too, or the condition still has a degree of freedom when it is read.

