-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY HALVING D IS MORE EXPENSIVE THAN IT LOOKS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
**Every comparison in this design is paired on D's items.** Both primaries need D:
A-D and D-C. So restricting D to one band does not halve one arm -- it restricts
THE WHOLE EXPERIMENT to that band. Arm A and arm C would still hold 349 items each,
and 174 of them would be unusable for either primary.

The cost lands on the only thing this design has to offer. Its stated prediction is
that nothing differs, so its value is the TIGHTNESS OF THE NULL, and cutting the
sample loosens exactly that:

    42 blocks over 349 items   detectable difference ~2.5%
    24 blocks over 174 items   detectable difference ~3.3%   (ambiguous only)
    18 blocks over 175 items   detectable difference ~3.8%   (clean only)

A wider null is a weaker null. "No difference larger than 2.5%" is a claim about
the architecture; "no difference larger than 3.8%" is closer to a shrug. The
pre-registered prediction is that all arms are indistinguishable, and the entire
worth of confirming it lies in how small a difference could have been ruled out.

It also destroys the pre-registered band split of record 17, which reports the two
bands separately so that pooling is a measurement rather than an assumption. With D
on one band there is nothing to split.

None of that makes the cut wrong if the cost is genuinely prohibitive. It makes the
cut EXPENSIVE, and the record says so before the number arrives rather than after.
